  "notes": "{散|ち}る describes things scattering or falling apart, especially petals and leaves.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: ⟦{自動詞|じどうし}→自動詞：10977_jidoushi⟧ (intransitive)\n- Pair: ⟦{散|ち}らす→散らす：02288_chirasu⟧ (transitive, to scatter something)\n\nASPECT (ている):\n{散|ち}って⟦いる→いる：00495_iru⟧ indicates a state where petals/leaves have fallen and are scattered.\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS:\n- ⟦{桜|さくら}→桜：02212_sakura⟧⟦が→が：00051_ga⟧{散|ち}る: cherry blossoms fall\n- ⟦{花|はな}びら→花びら：13988_hanabira⟧⟦が→が：00051_ga⟧{散|ち}る: petals scatter\n- ⟦{落|お}ち{葉|ば}→落ち葉：14108_ochiba⟧⟦が→が：00051_ga⟧{散|ち}る: fallen leaves scatter\n- ⟦{人|ひと}→人：00476_hito⟧⟦が→が：00051_ga⟧{散|ち}る: people disperse\n\nCULTURAL NOTE:\n⟦{桜|さくら}→桜：02212_sakura⟧⟦が→が：00051_ga⟧{散|ち}る (cherry blossoms falling) is a significant image in Japanese aesthetics, symbolizing the transient beauty of life (⟦{無常|むじょう}→無常：13422_mujou⟧). The phrase {散|ち}り{際|ぎわ} (moment of falling) describes a graceful end.\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- ⟦{散歩|さんぽ}→散歩：02885_sanpo⟧: a walk (different kanji compound)\n- {散|ち}り{散|ぢ}り: scattered about",
